    "nx_testimonials": {
      "type": "noxis-testimonials",
      "blocks": {
        "t1": {
          "type": "testimonial",
          "settings": {
            "image_asset": "noxis-review-1.jpg",
            "placeholder_label": "Man in his 50s, selfie in the driver's seat at night, wearing the glasses",
            "text": "<p>Honestly, I almost sent them back. My first week was dry and clear and I couldn't tell much difference. Then it poured. Interstate, construction zone, every headlight bouncing off the wet road at me. That's the drive that usually turns into one bright smear and I'm guessing where my lane is. With these on, it just looked like rain. Subtle on a clear night, a real difference in the wet. Around here, that's the only night that counts.</p>",
            "name": "Gary M"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "testimonial",
          "settings": {
            "image_asset": "noxis-review-2.jpg",
            "placeholder_label": "Woman and her elderly mother in a car at night, both wearing the glasses",
            "text": "<p>I bought these for my mom, who's 74 and had started asking me to drive her to things. I tried a pair on to make sure they weren't junk and ended up keeping it. I'm 43, perfect eyes, and I'd decided the headache on the drive home was just part of driving now. It isn't. For me it's the few seconds after a car passes, when I used to see nothing. Mom's verdict: 'I can finally watch the road instead of the lights.'</p>",
            "name": "Lauren T"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "testimonial",
          "settings": {
            "image_asset": "noxis-review-3.jpg",
            "placeholder_label": "Older man in the driver's seat at night, city lights behind, wearing the glasses",
            "text": "<p>I quit driving at night two winters ago and told everyone it was my choice. It wasn't. I stopped making plans that ended after six, and then people stopped inviting me. My first real test was the beltway at seven, in traffic, the exact drive I'd have talked myself out of. I didn't slow down for a single oncoming car. They're not magic, bright lights are still bright. But I drove somewhere alone after dark for the first time in two years.</p>",
            "name": "Ron D"
          }
        }
      },
