    "nx_honest": {
      "type": "noxis-image-text",
      "settings": {
        "asset": "noxis-headlights.webp",
        "placeholder_label": "Oncoming car at night with two bright LED headlights",
        "ratio": "1 / 1",
        "image_first": true,
        "title": "We Won't Pretend They're Magic",
        "text": "<p>Any ad promising that <strong>headlights will disappear is lying to you</strong>. Good amber <strong>takes the worst edge off the glare</strong>, which sounds small until you've <strong>driven 40 minutes</strong> without being blinded once by an oncoming car. Most glasses like these fail because they're <strong>just colored plastic</strong>: a yellow tint turns <strong>white glare into yellow glare</strong>, and the whole category gets a bad name. <strong>Better, not perfect</strong>, and <strong>you'll feel it on your first drive</strong>.</p>",
        "padding_top": 36,
        "padding_bottom": 36
      }
    },
    "nx_faq": {
      "type": "noxis-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My eye doctor says these don't work. Who's right?",
            "answer": "<p>Honestly, a bit of both of us. Every lens filters some light, so on a pitch-black road with no traffic you're better off without them, and our instructions say so. What your doctor isn't measuring is how uncomfortable glare feels on a busy road full of LED headlights. That's what these are for. Try them for 30 days and trust your own drive.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Could I miss a pedestrian in dark clothes?",
            "answer": "<p>It's exactly the right question, and it's why we tell you to take them off on unlit roads. The lens filters the harsh blue end of the light instead of dimming everything, so signs, curbs and people stay visible wherever there's any light at all.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will they fit over my prescription glasses?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. That's what they're designed for: nothing to swap, no second prescription to pay for. They also work on their own or with contacts.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Aren't these just cheap yellow-tinted glasses?",
            "answer": "<p>No, and those cheap ones are the reason people ask. A plain yellow lens just colors the glare instead of cutting it, which is why so many people say theirs \"just made everything yellow.\"</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What if they don't help me?",
            "answer": "<p>Email us within 30 days and we'll refund the full amount. We'd rather you test them properly and send them back than never find out. The case is yours to keep either way.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
